# u-boot boot script for the thin-Linux Dom0 flavour (DOM0_OS=linux).
#
# Boots: Xen 4.22 + a thin Linux Dom0 (hardware domain, running from an
# initramfs) + the dom0less DomD that Xen builds from /chosen/domD. DomU and
# DomA are created later by the DomD toolstack, not as boot modules here.
#
# Note on the firmware mailbox and the VPU DMA window: placing a Dom0 RAM bank low
# (< 1 GiB) so the firmware mailbox DMA buffer reaches the VPU.
# Confirmed on hardware: mailbox token TX/RX is fine but
# the firmware never writes the DMA response payload — root cause is
# `dma_alloc_coherent` returning a buffer outside VPU's DMA window.
# bare-metal RPi5 puts CMA in the default `linux,cma` range 0-0x10000000
# (low 1 GiB) so the VPU can see it. That is not what this script does now; see the
# /reserved-memory discussion further down.

# =============================================================================
# BOARD RAM SIZE — 16 GiB (default) or 8 GiB SKU
# =============================================================================
# `setenv board_ram` below is SUBSTITUTED AT BUILD TIME by the xt-rpi-u-boot-scr
# bbappend from ${BOARD_RAM} (moulin --BOARD_RAM 16g|8g, build.sh --ram=16g|8g). It
# is a plain env var, so it can also be overridden at the U-Boot prompt for a
# one-off test (no SD write): the assignment below is unconditional, so presetting
# board_ram does NOT work -- load the other script instead:
#     fatload mmc 0 ${scriptaddr} boot.scr.8g ; source ${scriptaddr}
#
# WHY A SWITCH IS NEEDED. The default domain map is sized for the 16 GiB board,
# which the firmware reports as total_memory=16372 (see the comment block in
# bcm2712-raspberrypi5-xen.dtso):
#     Dom0  512 + DomD 4096 + DomU 1024 + DomA 4096 =  9728 MiB  (+ Xen ~64)
# The static-mem banks reach 0x1c0000000 (7 GiB), so on an 8 GiB board the
# ADDRESSES all still exist -- what does not fit is the total. board_ram=8g
# therefore shrinks DomD and leaves every other domain alone:
#     Dom0  512 + DomD 3072 + DomU 1024 + DomA 3072 =  7680 MiB
#
# The reduction is SPLIT between DomD and DomA, and that split is measured, not a
# preference. An earlier revision of this script shrank DomD alone to 2048 and left
# DomA at 4096. bcm2712-raspberrypi5-xen.dtso had already recorded 2048 as a size
# measured to fail -- `xl devd` OOM-killed with both guests' qemu device-models plus
# weston and the VHAL backend, which is precisely the 4-domain case, and the reason
# DomD went to 4096 in the first place -- and reintroducing it reproduced the failure
# on hardware (2026-08-03): AAOS came up far enough to DHCP and then crash-looped in
# binder ("cannot find target node" / "undelivered transaction, process died", 16
# occurrences) and never set sys.boot_completed. Notably DomD still had 1.3 GiB free
# with no OOM kill and xendriverdomain alive, so the binding limit is not DomD's RAM
# but what its device model can map of a 4 GiB guest. DomD 3072 + DomA 3072 boots all
# four domains cleanly (0 binder errors, sys.boot_completed=1, both panels lit).
#
# DomA is therefore board-dependent too: xt-xen-cfg-doma substitutes doma.cfg's
# `memory` from BOARD_RAM (16g -> 4096, 8g -> 3072).
#
# 7680 + Xen ~64 = 7744 MiB against roughly 8180 MiB usable (extrapolated from the
# 16 GiB board's total_memory=16372 = 16384 - 12), i.e. about 436 MiB of headroom.
# That margin only exists because Dom0 is 512M rather than 1024M; at 1024M the same
# four domains came to 8256 MiB and over-committed the board. The 8180 figure is
# EXTRAPOLATED -- no 8 GB Pi 5 has been measured here.
#
# Dom0 is NOT board-dependent: dom0_mem=512M is the same on both SKUs. The value
# still decides WHERE bank[0] lands (allocate_memory_11 is not DT-pinnable), and the
# zephyr flavour relinks Zephyr's SRAM base to that address -- so the two scripts must
# keep the same dom0_mem. The two do NOT load the same modules (this one also fatloads
# the Dom0 initramfs at 0xa0000000), so bank[0] differs between the flavours --
# 0xa0000000 for zephyr, 0xc0000000 here. That is fine: only the zephyr flavour pins an
# absolute link address. tools/check-memory-map.py predicts bank[0] per flavour and
# checks the sram0 relink against the ZEPHYR one. See the dom0_mem discussion below.
setenv board_ram BOARD_RAM_PLACEHOLDER

fdt addr ${fdt_addr}
# 32768 bytes of FDT headroom, not the 8192 that is usually enough. With 8192,
# `fdt mknode` succeeded but the following `fdt set` calls on
# /chosen/domD/module@0 and @1 plus the long bootargs string hit FDT_ERR_NOSPACE,
# which the U-Boot Hush parser reports as "syntax error" and the boot fails. The
# properties added below need the extra room: two module entries at 32 bytes each,
# a bootargs string over 180 characters, and compatible stringlists over 40.
fdt resize 32768

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Apply the Xen device-tree overlay (creates /chosen/dom0).
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
fatload mmc 0 0x2200000 bcm2712-raspberrypi5-xen.dtbo
fdt apply 0x2200000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8 GiB SKU: shrink DomD from 4096 to 2048 MiB.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Must come AFTER `fdt apply`: /chosen/domD is created by the overlay, so a
# `fdt set` before this point would fail with FDT_ERR_NOTFOUND.
#
# Only the two HIGH banks change; the three low banks are untouched, which
# matters for two reasons:
#   * bank[0] = 0x50000000 is where Xen's place_modules() puts the DomD kernel and
#     DTB. Keeping it preserves the sizing that was verified on the 16 GiB board.
#   * 0x20000000 and 0x80000000 are inside the VPU's 32-bit DMA window and carry
#     vc4's CMA. Moving them would take CMA out of that window.
# No U-Boot module load lives in the freed range either -- every fatload in this
# script targets below 4 GiB (xen@0x2000000, Dom0 Image@0x4000000,
# domd.dtb@0xa000000, DomD Image@0xc000000), so dropping bank4 and halving bank3
# cannot collide with a load address. The highest static-mem address drops from
# 0x1c0000000 (7 GiB) to 0x140000000 (5 GiB).
#
#   0x50000000  +0x18000000  ( 384 MiB)  kept
#   0x20000000  +0x08000000  ( 128 MiB)  kept
#   0x80000000  +0x20000000  ( 512 MiB)  kept
#   0x100000000 +0x40000000  (1024 MiB)  bank3: 2 GiB -> 1 GiB
#   0x180000000 +0x40000000  (1024 MiB)  bank4: dropped
#                             = 2048 MiB  -> memory = <0x0 0x200000> (KiB)
#
# `test` is always available: cmd/Makefile has `obj-$(CONFIG_HUSH_PARSER) += test.o`,
# and the u-boot this build produces really does set it -- verified in the built
# config, not assumed:
#   tmp/work/raspberrypi5-poky-linux/u-boot/2024.04/build/.config
#     CONFIG_HUSH_PARSER=y
#     CONFIG_HUSH_OLD_PARSER=y
# So there is no CONFIG_CMD_TEST to forget. That matters because a missing `test`
# would make the condition fail and silently select the 16 GiB map on an 8 GiB board.
if test "${board_ram}" = "8g" ; then
    echo "board_ram=8g: DomD 3072 MiB (bank4 dropped)"
    fdt set /chosen/domD memory <0x0 0x300000>
    fdt set /chosen/domD xen,static-mem <0x0 0x50000000 0x0 0x18000000 0x0 0x20000000 0x0 0x8000000 0x0 0x80000000 0x0 0x20000000 0x1 0x0 0x0 0x80000000>
fi
echo "board_ram=${board_ram} dom0_mem=512M (placement-driven, board-independent)"

# Enable the dom0less DomD: Xen creates the Linux DomD at boot, with no toolstack.
# Linux Dom0 = control plane (no xl; DomD is dom0less).
# Keep the /chosen/domD slot + add direct-map + static-mem properties so DomD
# memory is 1:1 mapped, making the mailbox / V3D VPU DMA work.
#
# Effect: the XENFEAT_direct_mapped feature is set -> arm64 Linux's
# xen_swiotlb_detect() returns 1 -> xen_swiotlb_init() runs -> DMA address
# translation works via the grant table -> raspberrypi-clk probe SUCCESS.
fdt rm /chosen/xenpolicy   # xenpolicy slot not needed (XSM dummy is used)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Strip the 8 xen,passthrough markers the overlay added.
#
# The xen.dtbo was designed for the dom0less Hyperlaunch topology
# where DomD (not Dom0) owns these devices. In traditional Dom0
# mode the hardware domain (= Dom0) must own them, so the
# passthrough markers must be removed or `handle_device()` will
# set skip_mapping=true and Dom0 will never see the MMIO.
#
# these 8 devices are the
# AON GPIO controller plus the entire RP1 chip + brcm-pcie host
# bridge, all required for the SD card / LEDs / WiFi GPIO chain.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# [linux flavour — C3 fix (b)] The SoC SDHCI (mmc@fff000) + AON
# GPIO (gpio@7d517c00) are deliberately NOT passed through here: this thin Linux Dom0
# is the hardware domain and KEEPS the SD (it owns /dev/mmcblk0). In traditional-hwdom
# mode Xen assigns any node lacking a xen,passthrough marker to Dom0, and the xen.dtbo
# overlay carries no SDHCI marker, so simply not marking it leaves the SDHCI (and its
# IRQ SPI273) with Dom0-Linux.
#   - The dom0less DomD's partial DT (domd-vc4) OMITS the SD/GPIO block for
#     DOM0_OS=linux (domd-vc4.bb strips it), so DomD does NOT re-request SPI273 ->
#     no route_irq_to_guest -EBUSY.
#   - Dom0-Linux mounts /dev/mmcblk0p1 for the guest kernels and block-attaches p3/p4
#     to DomD as xvdb/xvdc (xl-attach-disks.service); the Dom0-thin image's
#     linux_dom0_guest_disks sed points DomU/DomA qemu -drive at xvdb/xvdc.
# (The zephyr flavour instead marks mmc@fff000 passthrough in its own boot.cmd so its
#  DomD owns the SD -- that file is separate and unchanged. A prior C3 "fix (a)" wrongly
#  added that passthrough here too, which broke the Linux hwdom's SD ownership.)
# bcm_reset (@1504318) + pcie_rescal (@119500) are NO LONGER stripped here: they
# move to DomD with the PCIe RC (RP1 -> DomD, toward Zephyr-Dom0) so DomD's
# brcm-pcie can reset the link. They are marked xen,passthrough in the RP1 block
# near the end of this script.

# Do NOT hand PCIe2 to Dom0. Root cause of a silent Xen reset: removing
# `xen,passthrough` from the PCIe2 host bridge (/axi/pcie@1000120000) gives it to
# Dom0 -> Xen tries to 1:1 map all regions of bcm2712.dtsi's PCIe2 ranges property
# (16 GiB MMIO: 4 GiB 32-bit @ 0x1f00000000 + 12 GiB 64-bit @ 0x1c00000000) via
# `map_device_children` in `prepare_dtb_hwdom` -> `dt_for_each_range` ->
# `p2m_set_entry`. On a debug build (CONFIG_DEBUG/VERBOSE_DEBUG/DEBUG_LOCKS/
# SCRUB_DEBUG) + `loglvl=all` + the 0007-0018 patches' verbose trace, a
# non-deterministic silent reset occurred during device assignment (reproduced
# twice, with no trap message just before the reset -> via a
# BCM2712-internal brown-out or EL3 sync abort).
#
# Dom0 is the control plane and is managed over the UART / hvc0 console. It gives
# up SSH over the RP1 ethernet (end0) as a result, but SD boot and the console both
# run on BCM2712 SoC-internal blocks (SDHCI and PL011), so Dom0 still boots. PCIe2
# and the whole RP1 chip are passed through to DomD instead.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Disable the legacy bcm2708_fb device-tree node.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
fdt rm /soc@107c000000/fb

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# /reserved-memory is passed through unmodified. This is a deliberate decision with
# two parts.
#
# First, Dom0 RAM is NOT forced into the low 1 GiB. Shrinking dom0_mem does not move
# it there, because Xen's allocate_memory_11() is strictly top-down; the only thing
# that moved it was reserving 0x10000000-0xffffffff as a no-map /reserved-memory
# entry so Xen fell back to the low free holes. That is not needed here: Dom0 does
# not touch the firmware mailbox in this topology, and Dom0 is allocated direct-map
# 1:1 in the upper part of low memory. This flavour also fatloads its Dom0 initramfs
# at 0xa0000000, which takes that candidate out of play, so bank[0] lands at
# 0xc0000000 here while the zephyr flavour gets 0xa0000000 (both measured). A Linux
# Dom0 does not care: the arm64 Image is relocatable and runs wherever bank[0] is.
#
# Second, /reserved-memory must not be rewritten to #size-cells=1. Doing so is fatal
# for a 6.18 Dom0:
#   the root node has #size-cells=2, so a /reserved-memory with #size-cells=1 is a
#   cells mismatch. Linux's __reserved_mem_check_root then logs "OF: reserved mem:
#   unsupported node format, ignoring" and ignores /reserved-memory entirely. atf@0
#   (0x0-0x80000, no-map) is therefore not reserved, Dom0 treats the atf@0 that Xen
#   writes into its second /memory node as Normal RAM, allocates gpa 0x2280 while
#   unpacking the initramfs, issues a DC ZVA to an unmapped p2m and dies on a
#   stage-2 alignment fault (DFSC=0x21) about one second into boot.
#
# Xen parses the 4-cell form correctly (device_tree_get_meminfo uses the node's own
# #address-cells/#size-cells), so passing the host default through -- #size-cells=2
# with atf@0 as <0 0 0 0x80000> no-map -- is both correct and sufficient: Dom0
# reserves atf@0 as no-map and the ATF region stays protected under Xen and Dom0.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

# No xen-block-high reservation is created here. A no-map reservation of
# 0x10000000-0xffffffff (3 GiB) overlaps the overlay's xen,static-mem region 1
# (0x50000000-0x68000000): assign_static_memory_11() then tries to acquire pages
# that are already marked used, acquire_domstatic_pages fails, and Xen panics with
# no message. Since Dom0 does not need to be pushed into low memory in this
# topology, the reservation is simply absent and DomD's static-mem regions can be
# acquired.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keep IOMMU mode: the `iommus` properties of /hvs@107c580000 and /axi/gpu are NOT
# removed.
#
# Removing them switches those devices to direct DMA, and then the dma_addr the vc4
# driver writes into the HVS display list does not carry the VPU bus offset
# (+0x10_00000000): dma_alloc returns a CPU physical address, /axi's dma-ranges is
# not applied during the parent walk, and HVS reads an invalid address. The visible
# result is strong vertical striping across the whole screen and the GL test triangle
# disappearing.
#
# Two candidate fixes exist for making direct DMA work, and neither is applied here
# because both are large changes that need their own hardware validation:
#   1. a vc4 driver change passing v3d_node (/axi/v3d, which inherits the parent
#      /axi dma-ranges) to of_dma_configure
#   2. large-page coalescing in bcm2712_iommu
# Known limitation of IOMMU mode: some residual visual noise remains, but weston
# renders correctly.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Disable the Bluetooth UART: nothing in this configuration uses it.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
fdt set /soc@107c000000/serial@7d50c000 status "disabled"

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why every GPU-related node below is marked xen,passthrough.
#
# Observed symptom: `xl create` for the GPU domain with only `irqs=[33]` returned
# ENODEV, and the `iomem` HVS range returned EPERM. Root cause:
#
#   Xen ARM `handle_device()` (xen/arch/arm/device.c:307-392) **ignores** the DT
#   `status="disabled"` and decides passthrough solely on the presence of the
#   `xen,passthrough` property. status=disabled stops the Linux driver probe, but
#   Xen still assigns the device + routes the IRQ to Dom0. Then the xl create for
#   DomD tries to bind the same IRQ, and `route_irq_to_guest` returns **-EBUSY**
#   for `desc->action != NULL && _IRQ_GUEST && d != ad` (shown as ENODEV by the
#   libxl wrapper).
#
# Solution: add a `xen,passthrough` property to each GPU-related node. Then Xen
#   decides `dt_device_for_passthrough(dev) = true` and skips IRQ/MMIO assignment
#   to Dom0 -> DomD can bind/grant.
#   (Xen automatically writes status="disabled" into the Dom0 DT, so a separate
#   status set is unnecessary.)
#
# Target nodes:
#   - mailbox + firmware
#   - HVS + V3D + HDMI x2
#   - disp_intr (HVS's L2 interrupt parent, SPI 97)
#   - aon_intr (HDMI's L2 interrupt parent, SPI 239)
#     Unless the parent L2 intc is also passed through, the child IRQ is held by Dom0.
#
# The node paths below are not interchangeable and must be written exactly as given:
# hvs sits at the root level while v3d sits under the axi bus. Getting one wrong is
# easy to miss, because `fdt set` on a non-existent path only prints
# `libfdt fdt_path_offset() returned FDT_ERR_NOTFOUND` and the U-Boot Hush parser
# does not abort the rest of the script -- so a single mistyped path silently leaves
# that one device assigned to Dom0.
#
# Exact paths, taken from a decompile of the built bcm2712-rpi-5-b.dtb:
#   /hvs@107c580000             <- at root level
#   /axi/v3d@2000000            <- under /axi (not at root level)
#   /soc@107c000000/mailbox@7c013880
#   /soc@107c000000/firmware
#   /soc@107c000000/hdmi@7c701400
#   /soc@107c000000/hdmi@7c706400
#   /soc@107c000000/interrupt-controller@7c502000  (disp_intr SPI 97)
#   /soc@107c000000/interrupt-controller@7d510600  (aon_intr SPI 239)
echo "passthrough: /soc/mailbox"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/mailbox@7c013880 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /soc/firmware"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/firmware xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /hvs"
fdt set /hvs@107c580000 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /axi/v3d"
fdt set /axi/v3d@2000000 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /soc/hdmi0"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/hdmi@7c701400 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /soc/hdmi1"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/hdmi@7c706400 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /soc/disp_intr"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/interrupt-controller@7c502000 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /soc/aon_intr"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/interrupt-controller@7d510600 xen,passthrough
# bsc_irq (intc@7d508380, GIC SPI 242 = absolute IRQ 274) must be passed through
# here as well as claimed in the DomD DT. If only the DomD DT claims it, Dom0 takes
# IRQ 274 via setup_irq() and DomD then panics with -EBUSY in route_irq_to_guest.
# bsc_irq is the L2 parent of ddc0/ddc1 (sub-IRQ 1 and 2) and is required for the
# HDMI EDID readback path.
echo "passthrough: /soc/bsc_irq (intc@7d508380, L2 intc for ddc i2c)"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/interrupt-controller@7d508380 xen,passthrough
# The IOMMU cache controller is passed through too. It has no interrupts, so there
# is no IRQ conflict to avoid; the reason is to stop Dom0 from ioremap'ing its MMIO
# and to keep ownership consistent with the IOMMU instance DomD uses.
# NOTE: the IOMMU instance DomD actually claims is /axi/iommu@5200 (domd-vc4 iommu4,
# xen,path = "/axi/iommu@5200"), which DomD receives via its static xen,reg mapping;
# only the sibling cache controller /axi/iommuc@5b00 is host-DT-passthrough'd here.
# iommu@5200 itself is not additionally marked, so Dom0 retains that node while DomD's
# static mapping is authoritative -- a benign retained-node situation (no IRQ, HW-proven
# on zephyr). Marking /axi/iommu@5200 for strict single-owner ownership is a candidate
# follow-up that needs on-hardware re-verification.
echo "passthrough: /axi/iommuc (BCM2712 IOMMU cache controller)"
fdt set /axi/iommuc@5b00 xen,passthrough
# PixelValve0/1 are passed through as well. Without this they stay active-routed in
# Dom0 at boot, so binding their IRQs (133 and 142) to DomD fails with EBUSY. The
# PixelValve is the CRTC pixel-stream generation stage of the HDMI output path: while
# Dom0 holds it, vc4-drm in DomD cannot take over HDMI and the Dom0 framebuffer stays
# on screen. SPI 101 -> absolute IRQ 133 (PV0), SPI 110 -> absolute IRQ 142 (PV1).
echo "passthrough: /soc/pixelvalve@7c410000"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/pixelvalve@7c410000 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /soc/pixelvalve@7c411000"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/pixelvalve@7c411000 xen,passthrough
# dvp (Display VPU clock + reset controller).
# Provider of the HDMI driver's audio (&dvp 0) + reset (&dvp 1|2).
echo "passthrough: /soc/clock@7c700000 (dvp)"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/clock@7c700000 xen,passthrough
# MOP/MOPlet (avoids the
# vc4_plane_create_additional_planes NULL deref TXP) + ddc i2c (HDMI EDID readback;
# requires driver bind via status="okay").
echo "passthrough: /soc/mop@7c500000"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/mop@7c500000 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /soc/moplet@7c501000"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/moplet@7c501000 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /soc/i2c@7d508200 (ddc0)"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/i2c@7d508200 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /soc/i2c@7d508280 (ddc1)"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/i2c@7d508280 xen,passthrough

# [native FAN] Pass through the SoC AVS thermal sensor to DomD.
# The RPi5 FAN (cooling_fan/pwm-fan) sits under RP1 and is already owned by DomD,
# but the temperature sensor (avs_monitor = brcm,bcm2711-thermal) was on the SoC
# side and traditionally owned by Dom0. Pass it to DomD and do the same native
# automatic FAN control via the DomD kernel's thermal-zone (cpu_thermal) +
# cooling-map (userspace daemon removed). avs_monitor has zero clocks/power-
# domain/reset dependency, only syscon MMIO (0x10_7d542000/4KB), and no interrupt.
# Dom0 is thin and does not use thermal + cooling_fan (RP1) is already passed
# through, so taking it is harmless (Xen just sets status=disabled in the Dom0 DT).
# Paired with the DomD partial DT's avs-monitor@107d542000 (xen,reg) + cfg iomem "0x107d542,1".
echo "passthrough: /soc/avs-monitor@7d542000 (SoC AVS thermal sensor for DomD FAN)"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/avs-monitor@7d542000 xen,passthrough

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# RP1 (USB touch + Ethernet) -> DomD, toward the Zephyr-Dom0 architecture.
# Mark the PCIe RC, the MSI controller, the RP1 sub-devices and the two PCIe
# reset controllers xen,passthrough (same fdt-set pattern as the GPU devices
# above). Xen then withholds them from Dom0; DomD owns them via domd.cfg
# iomem/irqs + the DomD partial DT (rp1 subtree + the pcie2 0x1f aperture).
#
# This does not reintroduce the silent reset described further up. Handing pcie2 to
# DomD (a domU) goes through the xen,reg path, which maps ONLY the explicit RP1
# aperture at host 0x1f_00000000 -- not the 16 GiB pcie2 `ranges`. The silent reset
# came from the Dom0-hwdom path (prepare_dtb_hwdom -> map_device_children) mapping
# those ranges, and that path only runs while Dom0 owns pcie2.
#
# Dom0 loses end0/eth0 -> manage via UART; restore later via DomD netback.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
echo "passthrough: /axi/pcie@1000120000 (PCIe RC / RP1)"
fdt set /axi/pcie@1000120000 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /axi/msi-controller@1000130000 (mip0 MSI)"
fdt set /axi/msi-controller@1000130000 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /axi/pcie@1000120000/rp1/ethernet@100000"
fdt set /axi/pcie@1000120000/rp1/ethernet@100000 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /axi/pcie@1000120000/rp1/usb@200000"
fdt set /axi/pcie@1000120000/rp1/usb@200000 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /axi/pcie@1000120000/rp1/usb@300000 (touch)"
fdt set /axi/pcie@1000120000/rp1/usb@300000 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /axi/pcie@1000120000/rp1/gpio@d0000"
fdt set /axi/pcie@1000120000/rp1/gpio@d0000 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /axi/pcie@1000120000/rp1/clocks@18000"
fdt set /axi/pcie@1000120000/rp1/clocks@18000 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /soc/reset-controller@1504318 (bcm_reset)"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/reset-controller@1504318 xen,passthrough
echo "passthrough: /soc/reset-controller@119500 (pcie_rescal)"
fdt set /soc@107c000000/reset-controller@119500 xen,passthrough

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Remove the VPU dynamically-injected framebuffer node from the Dom0 DT. At RPi5 boot the VPU (closed firmware) dynamically injects a
# simple-framebuffer node into /chosen/framebuffer etc. -> the Dom0 simplefb driver
# registers fb0 -> fbcon grabs the console and holds the HDMI output (C pattern).
# Even when DomD vc4-drm tries to take HDMI control, the display via Dom0 fb0
# remains. Each `fdt rm` is best-effort; lines with a missing path are skipped by
# the U-Boot Hush parser (no chain abort).
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
echo "rm: /chosen/framebuffer (VPU dynamic-inject)"
fdt rm /chosen/framebuffer
echo "rm: /chosen/framebuffer@3f800000"
fdt rm /chosen/framebuffer@3f800000
echo "rm: /reserved-memory/framebuffer"
fdt rm /reserved-memory/framebuffer
echo "rm: /framebuffer@3f800000"
fdt rm /framebuffer@3f800000

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Xen hypervisor bootargs.
#
# dom0_mem = 512M, matching the zephyr flavour. See the detailed sizing/placement
# note further down: the recorded 256M/512M failures were measured when DOM0 ITSELF
# RAN WESTON, which this thin image no longer does, and the value additionally
# decides where bank[0] lands (0xa0000000 at 512M, MEASURED), which the zephyr
# flavour's SRAM relink depends on -- so both scripts must carry the same value.
#
# For reference, if a high-memory reservation is combined with this, Xen's
# allocate_memory_11() splits the allocation into 3 low banks + 1 high bank:
#  - BANK[0] 0x10000000-0x18000000 (128 MiB, ex-Zephyr-Dom0 slot)
#  - BANK[1] 0x20000000-0x28000000 (128 MiB)
#  - BANK[2] 0x30000000-0x38000000 (128 MiB)
#  - BANK[3] 0x1e8000000-0x1f0000000 (128 MiB at 8 GiB area)
#
# The 3 low-memory banks are within the VPU 32-bit DMA window, so the mailbox keeps
# working. The 4th bank is VPU-accessible only via the IOMMU (high address).
# All weston / vc4 buffers are allocated from low memory.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Individual arguments:
#
#   xsm=dummy -- Flask is disabled outright, not merely set permissive. Two reasons.
#     Its hardened rules deny use_noiommu even under flask=permissive, which blocks
#     iomem passthrough on BCM2712. And a dom0less domain has no way to be given a
#     seclabel: dom0less-build.c parses no XSM label from the DT, so such a domain is
#     always unlabeled_t, and constructing DomD failed with
#     `avc: denied {create} xenboot_t->unlabeled_t domain` -> `Could not set up
#     domain domD (rc=-3)` -> Xen panic. Running Flask in production would require
#     adding xen_build_domain(domD_t) to the policy first.
#
#   dom0_mem=512M. The recorded failures (512M -> 18 MB free -> systemd OOM-killed
#     and a corrupt `xl list` entry; 2048M exhausted once DomU was added because the
#     xl process managing xenstore and three domains grew past 1 GB) were all
#     measured when DOM0 RAN WESTON. This image does not: rpi5-image-xt-dom0-thin.bb
#     PACKAGE_EXCLUDEs weston/mesa/wayland/qemu/python3, and its rootfs is the
#     initramfs, measured at 23 MB gzip / 65 MiB unpacked into tmpfs. At 1024M the
#     Dom0 kernel reported `Memory: 946696K/1085952K available (... 127816K
#     reserved ...)`, i.e. ~141 MiB of fixed overhead, so 512M leaves roughly 300 MiB
#     free after the tmpfs rootfs.
#     WARNING: NOT YET MEASURED ON HARDWARE at 512M. The unresolved item from the history
#     above is the "xl grew past 1 GB" claim: in THIS flavour Dom0 still runs xl and
#     xenstored, so if `xl create` of DomU/DomA fails or systemd is OOM-killed, this
#     is the first value to revisit. Check with `free -m` in Dom0 after each guest is
#     created. Reverting means restoring 1024M HERE AND the sram0 relink in
#     meta-xt-dom0-zephyr/0008-rpi5-board-domd-owns-sd.patch (0xa0000000 ->
#     0xc0000000) -- the two are coupled through bank[0] placement.
#
#   dom0_max_vcpus and gnttab_max_frames=512 follow the V4H SoDeV reference
#     (r8a779g3-xen-chosen.dtsi); gnttab_max_frames is sized for several guests.
#
#   sync_console -- synchronises console TX. It was added while investigating missed
#     UART RX events under console_lock contention; it is retained because it makes
#     console output ordering deterministic when several domains print at once.
fdt set /chosen xen,xen-bootargs "console=dtuart dtuart=/soc@107c000000/serial@7d001000 dom0_mem=512M dom0_max_vcpus=1 xsm=dummy gnttab_max_frames=512 bootscrub=off loglvl=all guest_loglvl=all sync_console"

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Load Xen
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
fatload mmc 0 0x2000000 xen
echo "Xen loaded at 0x2000000, size 0x${filesize}"

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Linux Dom0 bootargs
#
# Static IP via the kernel ip= argument so SSH works even when UART RX is broken.
# vc_mem.mem_base/size tell the vc_mem driver where the
# VPU memory region lives, in case any consumer relies on it.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
fatload mmc 0 0x4000000 Image
fdt set /chosen/dom0 reg <0x0 0x4000000 0x0 0x${filesize}>

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Boot DomD via dom0less + direct-map
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Load the DomD Image (linux-domd-vc4) and the DomD partial DT as multiboot modules.
# Set xen,direct-map + xen,static-mem on /chosen/domD to force 1:1 mapping.
# DomD memory range: host phys 0x100000000-0x140000000 (1 GiB, high address).
# Since it is direct-map, the guest gfn is the same 0x100000000-0x140000000 = mfn.

# First-boot graceful handling.
# If the DomD kernel / DTB are not yet placed on the SD boot partition (mmcblk0p1
# FAT32), fatload fails -> `setenv domd_loaded 1` does not run -> the
# `if test "${domd_loaded}"` branch fdt-rm's /chosen/domD and falls back to
# **Dom0-only boot**. This avoids a Xen panic on the auto-boot right after the first
# SD card write, and the auto DomD bring-up happens on the 2nd boot after deploy via
# install-domd-binaries-to-boot.sh.
#
# U-Boot Hush syntax: `if cmd ; then ... fi` branches on cmd's exit status
# (0=success; fatload returns non-zero when the file is absent).
#
# The DomD kernel loads at 0xc000000, not 0x8000000: the Xen DT overlay reserves
# 0x8000000 as optee_shm@8000000 (4 MB of reserved-memory), so U-Boot's LMB rejects
# the fatload there with `Reading file would overwrite reserved memory`. 0xc000000
# starts a 456 MB contiguous free region between optee_shm and optee_core
# (0x1d000000).
#
# The sizes below are literals, substituted at build time. The recipe's
# do_compile:append reads the actual DomD kernel and DTB sizes, seds them into the
# placeholders and runs mkimage. Using U-Boot environment expansion (${filesize},
# ${domd_kernel_size}) instead was a source of boot failures, because a value with a
# 0x prefix does not survive the expansion path reliably.
# Why DomD is dom0less and not created with `xl create`.
#
# `xl create` cannot give a domain a direct map -- libxl has no support for it. With
# GPA != PA the V3D's own MMU then walks invalid addresses, every GPU job hangs, the
# driver enters a reset loop and the display runs at about 1 fps. This was measured
# on hardware. A dom0less domain can be direct-mapped (GPA = PA), which makes the
# V3D MMU addresses match real physical addresses, so DomD is built by Xen at boot
# from /chosen/domD instead.
#
# The `xl create` route was tried, and it is the arrangement the V4H SoDeV reference
# uses (Dom0 runs a systemd unit that does `xl create -F /etc/xen/domd.cfg`, with
# driver_domain=1 and a Flask seclabel so privileged hypercalls such as xc_physinfo
# are permitted). It works, but not with a direct-mapped GPU domain, which this
# board needs.
# The overlay's (bcm2712-raspberrypi5-xen.dtbo) /chosen/domD already has `direct-map;`
# + xen,static-mem (3 banks, 1 GB total) + memory/cpus/vpl011/xen,enhanced, so here we
# add only module@0/1/2 + bootargs + domain-id (leave direct-map/static-mem to the
# overlay = do not hand-write the `xen,direct-map` prefix spelling that is invalid on xt0.2).
# Rootfs in this flavour: the thin Linux Dom0 is the hardware domain and keeps the
# SoC SDHCI, so DomD cannot read /dev/mmcblk0p2 itself (its partial DT omits the
# SD/GPIO block -- domd-vc4.bb strips it -- precisely so it does not re-request mmc
# IRQ SPI273 and hit route_irq_to_guest -EBUSY). DomD therefore takes p2 over Xen
# PV-block as /dev/xvda: xen-blkfront is built in (xen-frontend-force.cfg), the
# kernel blocks on rootwait, and Dom0's xl-attach-disks.service block-attaches
# phy:/dev/mmcblk0p2 as xvda once the toolstack is up. root= is the only difference
# from the zephyr flavour, which owns the SDHCI and mounts p2 directly.
# graceful: if the DomD kernel is not on SD p1, fdt-rm falls back to Dom0-only boot
# (avoids a panic before the first deploy).
# Note: with the dom0less switch, Dom0's domd.service (xl create DomD) is disabled.
if fatload mmc 0 0xc000000 Image ; then
    setenv domd_kernel_size ${filesize}
    # The GPU passthrough partial DTB. Its ddc0/ddc1/iommu4/iommuc/aon_intr xen,reg
    # entries must be page-aligned, and entries falling in the same page are merged
    # into one node so they do not overlap; otherwise Xen rejects the domain with
    # "not page aligned" (-22). With direct-map (GPA=PA) the V3D's
    # own MMU address matches the real PA -> aims to clear the V3D hang (1fps).
    # module layout: @0 kernel (Image) / @1 device-tree (partial DTB, GPU passthrough)
    # / @2 ramdisk.
    # Note: xl-create DomD (domd.cfg, same GPU iomem) fails for a later xl create
    # because the dom0less DomD (id1) claims the GPU first (id1 holds the GPU). Plan to
    # reliably disable domd.service later.
    fatload mmc 0 0xa000000 bcm2712-raspberrypi5-domd-vc4.dtb
    setenv domd_dtb_size ${filesize}
    fdt set /chosen/domD xen,domain-id <1>
    # step2 : grant DomD CDF_privileged via xen,privileged (patch 0056).
    # -> DomD's libxl/xl devd can run privileged sysctls, so the qemu device-model
    # (DomU virtio-gpu backend) can start. Without this, DomD's xl dies with
    # libxl_cpu_bitmap_alloc failed (step1 = confirmed on HW). Empty bool prop.
    fdt set /chosen/domD xen,privileged
    # [4.21 fix] In Xen 4.21 the dom0less parsing moved to
    # common/device-tree/dom0less-bindings.c, and the privileged spec changed from
    # xen,privileged (0056, targets arch/arm/dom0less-build.c, disabled in M1) to
    # capabilities=<DOMAIN_CAPS_CONTROL=0x1>. Without this DomD stays non-privileged,
    # so the device-model qemu's dm_op (IOCTL_PRIVCMD_DM_OP) is EPERM ->
    # xen_register_ioreq fails -> pcie.0 not created -> DomU/DomA black screen
    # (confirmed via HW strace).
    # Keep the xen,privileged above for 4.19 compatibility (a no-op ignored on 4.21).
    fdt set /chosen/domD capabilities <0x1>
    # cpus is left at the overlay default of 2. Raising it was tried and the second
    # vcpu's SMP bringup (PSCI CPU_ON) hung, stopping the domain before it reached
    # xenbus.
    # cpus=4 was tried, but DomD's xenstore init (xenbus_probe ->
    # xs_talkv) D-blocked forever (the event-channel-driven xenstore ring broke at
    # 4 vcpu; consistent with Xen q Inflight irq=65), so the readiness signal
    # (weston-up=ready) never raised and Dom0's xl-create-domu waited forever ->
    # neither DomU/DomA was created and both screens were black. Also DomD actually
    # ran at 100% on a single vcpu (other cores idle) = increasing cpus had no effect.
    # So revert to the overlay-default cpus (this line = remove the cpus override).
    # (Historical note: the 4-vCPU xenstore death described above was the
    # init-dom0less per-vcpu availability bug, fixed by xen-tools patch 0062;
    # the overlay default is 2 vCPUs since the dtso cpus=<2> change.)
    fdt mknode /chosen/domD module@0
    fdt set /chosen/domD/module@0 compatible "multiboot,kernel" "multiboot,module"
    fdt set /chosen/domD/module@0 reg <0x0 0xc000000 0x0 0x${domd_kernel_size}>
    # Do not add earlycon or ignore_loglevel here. With earlycon, the old bootargs'
    # `earlycon=pl011,0x22000000` + `ignore_loglevel` caused d1 to **infinitely spin
    # in pl011_putc during early boot** (console, not vc4!). xenconsoled could not
    # drain the emulated vpl011 TX FIFO fast enough, so FR.TXFF stayed full ->
    # earlycon's synchronous polling output spun at 100% CPU waiting for FIFO space ->
    # it never reached init-vc4/vc4 modprobe and HDMI = U-Boot logo. Remove earlycon
    # (synchronous flooder) + ignore_loglevel (excessive output). Normal
    # console=ttyAMA0 is drained after xenconsoled attaches, so it does not spin.
    # `quiet` is required as well; removing earlycon alone was not enough. Now the Xen PV console (hvc0) domU_write_console
    # infinitely looped on sched_op(yield) with the ring (4KB) full (because
    # xenconsoled does not drain the dom0less d1 console ring, the early-boot
    # KERN_INFO flood from hash tables etc. overflowed the ring). -> `quiet`
    # (console_loglevel=4, KERN_WARNING+ only) suppresses the early flood so the ring
    # does not overflow. Avoids the backpressure spin on both pl011/PV console.
    # cma=128M: originally 256M, halved to leave headroom for the RAM initramfs to
    # unpack. That reason is gone now that the root is p2 over PV-block, but the value
    # is kept because 128M is ample for the 1080p weston desktop and raising it back
    # would be an unmeasured change to the GPU allocator.
    # xen_privcmd.unrestricted=1: Linux 6.18's drivers/xen/privcmd.c restricts
    # hypercalls by default (auto-runs restrict_driver() when !xen_initial_domain()).
    # A dom0less DomD has no xenstore target node and is treated as DOMID_IDLE -> the
    # device-model qemu's IOCTL_PRIVCMD_DM_OP is rejected with EPERM, so it cannot
    # create pcie.0 and DomU/DomA fail to boot with `Bus 'pcie.0' not found` (6.12
    # works unrestricted). unrestricted=1 makes restrict_driver() early-return,
    # restoring the unrestricted dmop equivalent to 6.12.
    # root=/dev/xvda: Dom0 owns the SD in this flavour, so DomD's root arrives over
    # PV-block. rootwait makes the kernel block until Dom0's xl-attach-disks.service
    # has block-attached phy:/dev/mmcblk0p2 as xvda; rw is required (systemd and sshd
    # write to the root). There is no ramdisk module any more.
    fdt set /chosen/domD/module@0 bootargs "console=ttyAMA0 root=/dev/xvda rootwait rw clk_ignore_unused vhost_xen.nogrant=1 xen_privcmd.unrestricted=1 cma=128M net.ifnames=0 quiet loglevel=3"
    fdt mknode /chosen/domD module@1
    fdt set /chosen/domD/module@1 compatible "multiboot,device-tree" "multiboot,module"
    fdt set /chosen/domD/module@1 reg <0x0 0xa000000 0x0 0x${domd_dtb_size}>
    echo "DomD dom0less + direct-map + GPU passthrough configured (kernel@0xc000000 / dtb@0xa000000, root=/dev/xvda via Dom0 blkback)"
else
    fdt rm /chosen/domD
    echo "DomD kernel not on SD; Dom0-only boot (dom0less DomD disabled)"
fi
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# DomU = AGL Cluster IC (dom0less + xen,enhanced)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The node follows the same pattern as DomD's in the Xen DT overlay, including
# xen,enhanced = "enabled" (which turns on the dom0less xenstore and virtio paths)
# and xen,domain-p2m-iommu. Memory is given in KB, so <0x0 0x80000> is 512 MB.
#
# The DomU payload is a combined kernel + initramfs image.
# Load target: 0x40000000 (does not collide with DomD kernel 0xc000000 nor its dtb
# 0xa000000; 0x10000000, the old DomD initramfs slot, is now unused)
# Network: xen-netfront -> 192.168.10.12/24 via Dom0 xenbr0
#
# DomU is graceful: if the binary is absent, skip DomU startup and the rest (Dom0+DomD) still runs
#
# The DomU image load address is load-bearing. At 0x40000000 the image (326 MiB)
# runs to 0x5468b008 and overlaps DomD's xen,static-mem bank1
# (0x50000000-0x68000000) by 70.5 MiB. Xen's add_boot_module then fails
# check_reserved_regions_overlap, the module is not registered, create_domUs panics
# and the board hangs silently with no message.
#
# 0xa0000000 is used instead: it starts immediately after DomD's last static-mem
# bank (0x80000000-0xa0000000), ends at 0xb468b008 which is below the 4 GB boundary
# U-Boot can address, and overlaps none of DomD's banks (0x50000000-0x68000000,
# 0x20000000-0x28000000, 0x80000000-0xa0000000).
#
# DomU and DomA are created by the toolstack (`xl create`) rather than as dom0less
# domains: the dom0less path cannot be completed here because the qemu xenpvh
# backend attach needs Xen event-channel routing and a dom0less console pty in
# xenconsoled, and neither is available.
# (The dom0less DomU node construction that used to live here is removed: the
#  toolstack creates DomU, so the block was unreachable by construction.)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Linux Dom0 bootargs:
#   - Dom0 is the control plane and does not touch the GPU; DomD is the GPU driver
#   - modprobe.blacklist=vc4,v3d,simplefb: keep Dom0 off the GPU and off the
#     simple-framebuffer. simplefb has to be listed explicitly -- it is a separate
#     module and blacklisting vc4 does not stop it registering fb0.
#   - fbcon=map:99: map the console to a non-existent fb#99 -> prevent fbcon from
#     holding the HDMI output. This way the VPU framebuffer residue (C pattern) does
#     not persist until DomD vc4-drm's HDMI modeset.
#   - the legacy graphics cmdline (vc_mem, fbcon, bcm2708_fb) is intentionally absent
#   - no `ip=...:end0:off`: PCIe2 and the RP1 children are passed through to DomD, so
#     end0 (RP1 ethernet) does not exist in Dom0 at all. Passing it produces only a
#     device-absent warning and log noise.
# Dom0 is managed over the UART / hvc0 console (`console=hvc0 earlycon=xen`).
#
# SD layout:
#   p1 = boot     (512 MB vfat, required by the RPi5 boot ROM)
#   p2 = DomD rootfs (ext4)
#   p3 = DomU rootfs (ext4, AGL cluster)
#   p4 = DomA disk   (raw, AAOS)
#
# Dom0 itself is thin and runs from an initramfs (root=/dev/ram0) rather than from a
# partition, following the V4H SoDeV reference (r8a779g3-xen-chosen.dtsi).
fdt set /chosen/dom0 bootargs "console=hvc0 earlycon=xen earlyprintk=xen rdinit=/sbin/init ignore_loglevel modprobe.blacklist=vc4,v3d,simplefb fbcon=map:99"

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dom0 initramfs load (thin Dom0 boot)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The thin Dom0 rootfs is rpi5-image-xt-dom0-thin's cpio.gz, which must be present
# on SD p1 as initramfs-xt-dom0-thin.cpio.gz.
#
# It loads at 0xa0000000. 0x8000000 does not work: the initramfs is large enough to
# run past 0xc000000 and into the DomD/DomU reserved range (0xc000000-0x40000000),
# and fatload then refuses with "Reading file would overwrite reserved memory".
# 0xa0000000 is below the 4 GB boundary U-Boot can address.
fatload mmc 0 0xa0000000 initramfs-xt-dom0-thin.cpio.gz
setenv dom0_initramfs_size ${filesize}
echo "Dom0 initramfs loaded at 0xa0000000, size 0x${dom0_initramfs_size}"

# Set linux,initrd-start/end on DTS chosen/dom0 (= a direct hint to the Linux kernel).
# Note: compute the end address with setexpr (= start + size).
# U-Boot ${filesize} is a hex string with no 0x prefix (= "730f47b"). setexpr / fdt set
# fail to convert without the 0x prefix (= Fix #14; "Sorry, I could not convert" observed
# in a prior run).
setexpr dom0_initrd_end 0xa0000000 + 0x${dom0_initramfs_size}
fdt set /chosen/dom0 linux,initrd-start <0x0 0xa0000000>
fdt set /chosen/dom0 linux,initrd-end <0x0 ${dom0_initrd_end}>

# /chosen/dom0/module@1 must be declared as multiboot,ramdisk.
# With only linux,initrd-start/end, xen-troops dom0less does not hand the initrd to
# Dom0 -> the Linux kernel does not unpack the initramfs -> /dev/ram0 (= empty
# 4096 KB) mount fail -> "VFS: Unable to mount root fs on /dev/ram0" panic (observed
# in a prior run). Telling Xen about the initrd as a multiboot module makes Xen
# correctly set Dom0's /chosen/linux,initrd-start/end at dom0 boot.
fdt mknode /chosen/dom0 module@1
fdt set /chosen/dom0/module@1 compatible "multiboot,ramdisk" "multiboot,module"
fdt set /chosen/dom0/module@1 reg <0x0 0xa0000000 0x0 0x${dom0_initramfs_size}>

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Boot Xen → Xen hands off to Linux Dom0 (= thin initramfs base)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
setenv fdt_high 0xffffffffffffffff
booti 0x2000000 - ${fdt_addr}
